00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:11.440
It’s 4 am on a mid-August morning in the
northern California wilderness.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.150
During a brief overnight storm, lightning
strikes the top of a hill, ignites some dry

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.279
underbrush, and starts a wildfire.

00:00:18.279 --> 00:00:23.769
It isn’t until 6am as the sun rises that
someone notices the fire—a hiker camping

00:00:23.769 --> 00:00:25.050
an overlooking hill.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.930
As soon as the hiker’s 911 call is received
by the emergency dispatch center they contact

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:34.550
Cal Fire—the agency responsible for wildfire
protection and management in California.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:38.550
By 6:30, they call the Redding, California
based smokejumpers.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.309
They are one of most elite firefighting squads
in the United States.

00:00:42.309 --> 00:00:47.350
There are only 400 smokejumpers in the US
and 40 of those are based here in Redding,

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:48.350
California.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.420
They’re essentially the rapid response team
for wildfires.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.610
Within 15 minutes of receiving the call, these
smokejumpers are in their plane and taking

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:56.610
off.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:00.590
It takes just 20 minutes to fly the 50 miles
to the fire site where they find a suitable

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:05.010
tree-free landing spot, jump, deploy their
parachutes, and land.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:08.930
The plane then circles back to parachute down
boxes of equipment.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:13.690
Smokejumpers carry with them enough supplies
to last 72 hours completely self supported—food,

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:16.840
water, shelter, safety equipment, and firefighting
tools.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.000
They follow the ridge line and make their
way to the fire.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.510
Now, this is a small team and they need to
decide how to prioritize what they do.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:28.640
With the fire already having grown to 15 acres
the team knows they’re unlikely to stop

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.680
it on their own, backups are already on the
way, so their priority is to slow it down

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.470
as much as possible.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:39.140
There are four major factors that affect how
fast a fire moves: how much fuel there is,

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:42.400
how wet the fuel is, the wind direction, and
the slope.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.370
The two factors that the team can immediately
gauge that affect where the fire will move

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.610
the fastest are the wind direction and slope.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.140
In this case the wind is coming from the north
and they know that to the south-west is an

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.380
upward slope.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:58.150
Fire moves faster uphill than it does downhill
since fire burns upwards so these smokejumpers

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:01.720
know that this is likely the fire’s fastest
moving front.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:06.210
While trees do burn, the primary source of
fuel that a wildfire uses to move is the dry

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:10.750
brush and dead wood on the forest floor so
the biggest technique used to stop forest

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.680
fires is to create what’s called a fire
line.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.540
These are essentially a gap where they remove
all fuel—plants both dead and alive—so

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.890
that there’s nothing for the fire to continue
burning.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:25.570
The smokejumpers use a mix of chainsaws and
other hand tools to do this work but sometimes

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:27.480
fire lines are pre-built.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.500
In this case there’s a road at the top of
the uphill section which will help slow or

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.500
stop the fire so they can direct their efforts
elsewhere.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.830
They use the road as their anchor point—a
cleared section that the fire likely won’t

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:43.120
cross where they start building their fire
line so the fire can’t outflank them.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.140
Throughout this construction process the smokejumpers
need to be sure that they can escape in case

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.010
the winds shift or the fire picks up speed.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:54.470
Wildfires can move exceptionally fast—up
to 7 miles per hour in forests which is faster

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.610
than humans are often able to make their way
through dense trees.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:02.310
In grasslands fires can move up to 14 miles
per hour so firefighters have to be extra

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.310
cautious.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:07.430
For this reason firefighters rarely put themselves
directly in front of the fire’s moving front—they’ll

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.790
either be far ahead or to the side.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:13.070
In the case of this fire as they’re building
this first fire line they’re close to a

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:18.290
road which acts as an easy exit point but,
just in case, all smokejumpers carry fire

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:19.330
shelters.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:23.080
These compact, lightweight shelters won’t
survive direct flames for too long, but they

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.420
do greatly increase a firefighter’s chance
of survival in case they can’t escape the

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.930
path of the fire.

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:33.760
After a little over an hour of work the smokejumpers
complete a continuous fire line from the road

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:34.780
to a stream.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:39.209
Streams, while less secure than roads, also
help stop or slow down a fire by acting as

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:40.230
a fire line.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:44.000
To the south-east of the fire there’s also
a small road that connects to another stream

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.290
meaning there are at least rudimentary fire
lines on three sides of the fire.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:51.069
It’s at this time when backups arrive by
road.

00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:54.090
The added manpower allows for much faster
action.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:58.010
The immediate focus goes to strengthening
the southeastern fire line—currently just

00:03:58.010 --> 00:03:59.010
a stream.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:03.200
At closest the fire’s less than 100 feet
from the stream and the stream is itself in

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.200
a valley so it’s too risky to work directly
behind the stream, there’s just no good

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.030
escape route, so the new arrivals get to work
on a redundant fire line 100 feet behind the

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:13.030
stream.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:17.580
By 10 am the fire has further grown and the
team knows that the worst is still to come.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:22.820
Fires spread most rapidly between 10am and
sunset due to the daytime heat and wind.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:26.880
By 11am the fire has reached the road and
part of the fire line which means that some

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.139
of those that were building the fire-line
are re-allocated to make sure that the fire-line

00:04:31.139 --> 00:04:34.020
holds—extinguishing any flames that may
jump the road.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.180
By noon the fire lines are holding and, while
the fire’s size is growing, it’s at a

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:42.180
manageable pace so there’s reasonable hope
that it can be suppressed before expanding

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.070
to an uncontrollable size.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:46.780
At 1pm, though, conditions change.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:50.960
The wind starts blowing harder towards the
south-west and, as trees burn on the north

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.500
side of the road, the wind pushes embers over
the road which ignite underbrush on the other

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.720
side and now the entire focus of the firefighting
efforts change.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:04.940
You see, the point of fighting wildfires is
not actually to put them out, it’s to control

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:05.940
them.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.840
While the number of wildfires has increased
due to humans they’re actually a very natural

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:10.840
phenomenon.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.370
What’s making wildfires worse is humans
stopping them.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:19.180
Many forests survive wildfire through trees
having heat-resistant bark and other evolutionary

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:20.270
adaptations.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:24.800
These fire resistant forests relied on having
wildfires at a consistent interval every few

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.190
decades to clear out the forest floor of dead
plants and to kill invasive species.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:34.230
Nowadays, though, as humans suppress fires
a forest might only see a wildfire every 50

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:38.740
years instead of 25, for example, meaning
that there’s twice as much fuel and so the

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:41.880
fire burns faster, larger, and more intensely.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.820
It was only until recent years that the research
revealing this was widely accepted so the

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:50.290
goals of firefighting shifted from stopping
wildfires completely to controlling them.

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:55.010
In some places, being a firefighter actually
means starting fires.

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:59.630
Fire management agencies will conduct controlled
burns in order to reduce the risk of an uncontrollable

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:02.479
fire and to increase the health of a forest.

00:06:02.479 --> 00:06:07.229
While forests can survive wildfires, humans
cannot so most agencies will let fires burn

00:06:07.229 --> 00:06:11.290
in a controlled fashion up until the moment
they risk damaging property or threatening

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:12.680
human life.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:15.940
This fire that jumped the road just did exactly
that.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:20.040
At the bottom of this slope is a town and
the fire is now headed in that direction with

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.710
no preexisting features to stop it.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:26.620
That means that all measures must be taken
to aggressively stop the fire’s expansion

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:27.800
to the west.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:31.990
That means it’s time to bring out the big
guns—it’s time to attack the fire from

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.080
above.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:37.680
Planes and helicopters are some of the most
effective tools used to fight wildfires.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.040
They can quickly and accurately drop huge
amounts of water or fire retardant.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:46.479
The decision to use aerial firefighting does
not come lightly as it’s both hugely expensive

00:06:46.479 --> 00:06:50.360
and using an aircraft on one fire means it
can’t be used on another.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:53.630
Their use needs to be prioritized for the
most dangerous fires.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:58.270
There’s also a decision to be made on what
the aircraft is going to drop—water or flame

00:06:58.270 --> 00:06:59.460
retardant.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.710
Water is cheap and, with some aircraft designs,
can be reloaded near the fire without landing

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:05.070
at an airport.

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:08.210
Water is only effective at extinguishing flames,
though.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:12.050
Flame retardant, on the other hand, can be
used to stop flames from starting.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:16.100
It can essentially create a fire line ahead
of the fire’s spread as it will stop a line

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.630
of forest from burning.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:22.199
The main issues, though, are that flame retardant
can only be loaded at an airport and it’s

00:07:22.199 --> 00:07:23.360
very expensive.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.380
A gallon of Phos-Chek, the most commonly used
brand of flame retardant, costs $3.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:33.080
That’s about the same as a gallon of milk
at the grocery store but these aircraft use

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.690
thousands of gallons of it for each drop.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:40.919
The world’s largest firefighting aircraft,
the 747 supertanker, for example, carries

00:07:40.919 --> 00:07:46.710
19,600 gallons of flame retardant meaning
that what it uses in one drop costs nearly

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.740
$60,000.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:53.140
Of course there’s a reason agencies hire
this plane, it creates a 3 mile long fire

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:56.729
line almost instantly, but it comes at a steep
price.

00:07:56.729 --> 00:08:00.110
For this fire, using such an expensive tool
would be overkill.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:03.199
In this case, they’ll use a helicopter with
a bucket attached.

00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:07.210
The bucket is filled with water from a nearby
lake then the helicopter flies over to the

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:08.389
fire and drops it.

00:08:08.389 --> 00:08:13.169
With only 5 miles to the lake the helicopter
is able to make a drop about every 5-10 minutes

00:08:13.169 --> 00:08:17.830
and, while it works on stopping the spread
on one side hand crews work on building a

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.110
fire line on the other.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:23.600
The area that the helicopter extinguishes
essentially acts as its own fire line as fire

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:25.389
can’t burn what’s already burned.

00:08:25.389 --> 00:08:29.699
This work continues for the next few hours—its
vital to not let anything to the south-west

00:08:29.699 --> 00:08:31.550
of the road burn out of control.

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:35.909
By 4pm the outbreak is managed and attention
can be directed back to the largest section

00:08:35.909 --> 00:08:37.099
of the fire.

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:41.469
As the afternoon wears on some crews get back
to lengthening the eastern fire line, others

00:08:41.469 --> 00:08:45.839
begin constructing a western fire line, and
the helicopter works on slowing the advancement

00:08:45.839 --> 00:08:46.839
to the north.

00:08:46.839 --> 00:08:51.630
By the time the sun begins to set around 8,
all sides of the fire have at least some element

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:56.040
of control so that during the night and in
the coming days the fire can continue to burn

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:59.629
in a controlled fashion until there’s nothing
left to burn.

00:08:59.629 --> 00:09:04.510
This mission was a success but the reality
is that this is not a real fire.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:07.019
Real fires rarely end this well.

00:09:07.019 --> 00:09:12.089
Real fires don’t act so predictably because
real fires can’t be predicted.

00:09:12.089 --> 00:09:17.660
Real fires are a menace that can grow to the
size of small countries and can burn for months.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:22.360
During summer and fall, there are often more
than 100 large forest fires burning around

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:26.620
the US at any given time and thousands more
around the world.

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:30.940
No firefighting effort is exactly like another
but these are the primary techniques used

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:35.720
by those who work everyday to protect life
and property from one of nature’s most dangerous

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:37.709
phenomenons.

00:09:37.709 --> 00:09:41.670
Whether you’re fighting forest fires or
running a business the tools you use are crucially

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:42.670
important.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:47.220
In both cases they directly affect how well
you do your job and, while chainsaws, axes,

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:50.980
and shovels are some of the most important
tools in firefighting, Squarespace is one

